    "what_it_does_not_say": "This does not establish a bound. It is a marginal pass on one book, clearing by +0.0598. The pair rung carries 69 households, and at that size only a ceiling above +0.5529 can be told from chance at all -- so a real effect of moderate size is invisible to this instrument either way. The separate full-coverage rung (n=213) reads cannot tell on the same correction, and the two rungs disagreeing is itself a reason to hold the number loosely. And the winning fit scores 0.6127 on households it never saw against 0.1674 on the ones it was built from -- 3.7 times better out of sample than in it. A fit does not do that; a search over 45 candidates ranked by the out-of-sample score finds one that does. This is not evidence the ceiling is chance -- the winner of a shuffled world overshoots its own fit for the same reason -- so the p-value above stands. It is the one thing that p-value cannot see, and it is why this figure is reported as the best of a search rather than as a bound. AND THE VERDICT IS NOT A PROPERTY OF THE WORLD. Re-running the whole instrument -- the 45-way sweep and its own 200-draw corrected null -- on 32 consecutive run outputs of the SAME population at the same base seed returns 19 'clears' and 13 'cannot tell', p from 0.0249 to 0.0746, on rungs of 69 to 71 households. Those books are consecutive states of one population at one base seed, so the published answer moves with the run output the instrument read. A number that changes side on that is not a gate, and R3 and R4 must not be gated on it. And this is not noise around the line, which would settle with more draws. It is a step: every run at n=71 reads 'cannot tell' (ceiling +0.5661, p=0.0746); every run at n=69 reads 'clears' (ceiling +0.6127, p from 0.0249 to 0.0299). There is no scatter inside either group. The books either side of the step differ in the whole book too (213 against 214 households), not only in the rung, so which of the two moved the verdict cannot be attributed from this evidence and is not claimed here. Either way the published answer is decided by a difference of a couple of households in a book of over two hundred, which is not a quantity a programme can be gated on."
  }
}
